        session_number: 5,
        title: 'Facing Fears Step by Step',
        objective: 'Students will build a gradual exposure ladder to approach an avoided situation and take a first step.',
        opening_activity: 'Reframe share: what prediction did you challenge, and did the reframe change how you felt or acted?',
        main_activity: 'Explain the counterintuitive truth: avoidance shrinks your life while facing fears in small, repeated steps shrinks the fear. This works because your brain gathers new evidence that you can handle the situation. Model a "fear ladder" for a common example (speaking up in class): bottom rung is low-stakes (answer a question you are sure of), top rung is high-stakes (present to the whole class), with graded steps between. Each student builds their own ladder for one avoided situation, 6-8 rungs, ordered by anxiety rating. Emphasize staying on a rung until the anxiety drops before climbing. Students circle a realistic bottom rung to attempt this week.',
        closing_checkout: 'Each student states their bottom-rung step and when they will try it. Group offers encouragement.',
        take_home_challenge: 'Take your bottom-rung step at least once this week. Rate your anxiety before and after, and note what actually happened versus what you predicted.',
